window.AILETMOCK={"n":4,"title":"Mock Test 4","theme":"Governors and Article 356","kind":"Full-length paper","blurb":"Article 356, Governors, the right to health, choice of partner, judicial appointments and repugnancy in constitutional law. Seven more branches of law and contemporary legal GK complete the paper.","minutes":120,"neg":0.25,"sec":["Constitutional Law","Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","International Law and Human Rights","Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK"],"q":[{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Article 356","q":"In Rameshwar Prasad v. Union of India (2006), the Supreme Court held that the dissolution of the Bihar Legislative Assembly in 2005 was:","o":["unconstitutional, only because the Election Commission was not consulted","valid, since the President acted on the advice of the Cabinet promptly","valid, as the Governor's satisfaction is beyond judicial review altogether","unconstitutional, as the Governor's report was based on extraneous material"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that the Governor's report was mala fide and that Article 356 cannot be used to prevent a party from forming a government. It reaffirmed S.R. Bommai on judicial review of Article 356."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Governors","q":"In Nabam Rebia v. Deputy Speaker (2016), the Constitution Bench held that a Governor's power to summon or advance a legislative session:","o":["must ordinarily be exercised on the aid and advice of Ministers","is exercisable on the recommendation of the Election Commission only","is available only to the Speaker, and not to the Governor himself","is a discretionary power that need not follow any ministerial advice"],"a":0,"e":"Article 163(2) discretion is limited to cases where the Constitution expressly requires the Governor to act in his discretion; the Governor cannot otherwise act on his own to summon or advance sessions."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Broadcasting","q":"In Secretary, Ministry of I&B v. Cricket Association of Bengal (1995), the Supreme Court held that airwaves are:","o":["public property, and the right to telecast is part of Article 19(1)(a)","an area covered by Article 19(1)(g) alone, and not by Article 19(1)(a)","private property of broadcasters, outside the scope of any State regulation","the exclusive monopoly of the Union Government, with no citizen's right"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that the right to impart and receive information through broadcasting is part of free speech, though the State may regulate airwaves in public interest through an independent public authority."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Health","q":"In Paschim Banga Khet Mazdoor Samity v. State of West Bengal (1996), the Supreme Court held that failure of a government hospital to provide timely emergency medical treatment violates:","o":["the right to constitutional remedies under Article 32 alone","the right to life under Article 21","the freedom of movement under Article 19(1)(d)","the right to equality before law under Article 14 alone"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the State's constitutional duty to safeguard life extends to providing adequate medical facilities, and awarded compensation to the victim."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Choice of Partner","q":"In Shafin Jahan v. Asokan K.M. (2018), the Supreme Court set aside the annulment of an adult woman's marriage, holding that the choice of a life partner is part of:","o":["Articles 19 and 21, free from interference by family or courts","Article 26 alone, which is subject to the religion's customary law","Article 25 alone, which is subject to public order and morality","Article 14 alone, since it concerns equality between spouses"],"a":0,"e":"The Court stressed that an adult's ability to choose a spouse and faith is integral to autonomy and dignity, and the State and courts cannot substitute their own views."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Reservation","q":"In Ashoka Kumar Thakur v. Union of India (2008), the Supreme Court upheld the 93rd Amendment and the law reserving seats in central educational institutions for OBCs, subject to:","o":["a ceiling of ten per cent of the total seats","approval by two-thirds of the State Legislatures","exclusion of the creamy layer","a requirement of an annual census of backward classes"],"a":2,"e":"The Court upheld Article 15(5) and the Central Educational Institutions (Reservation in Admission) Act, 2006, but required exclusion of the creamy layer and periodic review of the backwardness of the classes."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Directive Principles","q":"Which Article of the Constitution directs the State to take steps to separate the judiciary from the executive in the public services of the State?","o":["Article 40","Article 48A","Article 51","Article 50"],"a":3,"e":"Article 50 is a Directive Principle on separation of judiciary from the executive, and it has been implemented through the Code of Criminal Procedure. Article 40 deals with village panchayats and Article 48A with environment."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Judicial Appointments","q":"The Third Judges Case (1998), decided on a Presidential Reference, held that the Collegium for appointments to the Supreme Court consists of:","o":["the Chief Justice of India and two senior-most Supreme Court judges","the Chief Justice of India, the Prime Minister and the Leader of Opposition","the Chief Justice of India and four senior-most Supreme Court judges","the Chief Justice of India and the Union Law Minister alone"],"a":2,"e":"The Second Judges Case (1993) had given primacy to the Chief Justice, in consultation with two senior judges. The Third Judges Case enlarged the Collegium to five."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Legislative Reservation","q":"The 104th Constitutional Amendment (2019) extended reservation of seats for Scheduled Castes and Scheduled Tribes in the Lok Sabha and State Assemblies up to:","o":["25 January 2040","25 January 2030","25 January 2025","25 January 2035"],"a":1,"e":"The same Amendment discontinued the nomination of Anglo-Indian members to the Lok Sabha and the State Assemblies, under Articles 331 and 333."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Repugnancy","q":"Under Article 254(2), a State law on a Concurrent List subject, though repugnant to an earlier Union law, prevails in that State if:","o":["it has been approved by the Governor after consulting the Union Cabinet","it has been ratified by at least half of the other State Legislatures","it has been reserved for the President and has received his assent","it has been passed by the State Legislature by a two-thirds majority"],"a":2,"e":"Parliament may, however, later enact a law on the same matter that adds to, amends, varies or repeals the State law (proviso to Article 254(2)). See M. Karunanidhi v. Union of India (1979)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Temple Entry","q":"Which Article enables the State to throw open Hindu religious institutions of a public character to all classes and sections of Hindus?","o":["Article 28(1)","Article 25(2)(b)","Article 26(b)","Article 30(1)"],"a":1,"e":"The Explanation to Article 25 states that references to Hindus include Sikhs, Jains and Buddhists. Article 26(b) gives religious denominations the right to manage their own affairs in matters of religion."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Minority Rights","q":"In T.M.A. Pai Foundation v. State of Karnataka (2002), the Supreme Court held that for the purposes of Article 30, a religious or linguistic minority is to be determined:","o":["by the Union Government, through a notification","district-wise, treating the district as the unit","country-wide, treating India as the unit","State-wise, treating the State as the unit"],"a":3,"e":"The eleven-judge bench held that linguistic minorities must be assessed by reference to the State because States were reorganised on a linguistic basis, and applied the same unit to religious minorities."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"President","q":"The impeachment of the President under Article 61 requires a resolution passed by:","o":["not less than two-thirds of the total membership of each House","a simple majority of the Lok Sabha membership acting by itself","three-fourths of the total membership of the Lok Sabha alone","two-thirds of the members present and voting in each of the Houses"],"a":0,"e":"The charge is preferred by one House on a notice signed by at least a quarter of its members and given 14 days earlier. The other House investigates, and the President may appear or be represented."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Finance","q":"Under Article 113(2), a demand for a grant can be made only on the recommendation of:","o":["the Speaker of the Lok Sabha","the Finance Minister","the Comptroller and Auditor-General","the President"],"a":3,"e":"No demand for a grant may be made except on the President's recommendation, and the Rajya Sabha has no power to vote on demands for grants. Money Bills require the President's recommendation under Article 117."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"CAG","q":"Under Article 148, the Comptroller and Auditor-General of India can be removed from office:","o":["by the Lok Sabha acting alone, through a resolution passed by a majority","in the like manner and on the like grounds as a Supreme Court judge","by the Public Accounts Committee, on a report to the Speaker","by the President at his pleasure on the advice of the Prime Minister"],"a":1,"e":"This ensures the independence of the CAG, who is appointed by the President and cannot hold any office under the Government after retirement."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Strike","q":"In T.K. Rangarajan v. Government of Tamil Nadu (2003), the Supreme Court held that government employees:","o":["have a fundamental right to strike, subject only to public order","have no fundamental, legal or moral right to strike","have a statutory right to strike under the Industrial Disputes Act","have a fundamental right to strike under Article 19(1)(c)"],"a":1,"e":"The Court upheld the dismissal of striking employees, noting that the right to form associations under Article 19(1)(c) does not include a guaranteed right to strike, which depends on statute."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Police Reforms","q":"In Prakash Singh v. Union of India (2006), the Supreme Court issued directions on police reforms that included:","o":["abolishing the post of the Director General of Police in every State","a ban on the use of firearms by State police forces in public places","a State Security Commission and a fixed minimum tenure for the DGP","transferring control of all State police forces to the Union Home Ministry"],"a":2,"e":"The directions also covered separation of investigation from law and order, a Police Establishment Board and Police Complaints Authorities."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Honour Killings","q":"In Shakti Vahini v. Union of India (2018), the Supreme Court held that the interference by khap panchayats in the marriage of consenting adults is:","o":["illegal, and preventive and punitive measures were directed","valid, if the khap panchayat has been recognised by the State","legal, as a social custom protected under Article 25 itself","legal, if both the families have agreed to the intervention"],"a":0,"e":"The Court directed States to identify vulnerable districts, set up special cells and ensure prompt action against honour crimes, and asked Parliament to consider a special law."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Federalism","q":"Under Article 249, Parliament may legislate on a matter in the State List in the national interest if the Rajya Sabha passes a resolution supported by:","o":["three-fourths of the members present and voting","two-thirds of the total membership of the House","a simple majority of the members present and voting","two-thirds of the members present and voting"],"a":3,"e":"A law made under Article 249 remains in force for a period not exceeding one year at a time after the resolution ceases to be in force, unless it is renewed by another resolution."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Emergency","q":"The 44th Amendment replaced the ground of 'internal disturbance' for a proclamation of national emergency under Article 352 with:","o":["civil unrest","external aggression","armed rebellion","public disorder"],"a":2,"e":"The grounds are now war, external aggression or armed rebellion. The proclamation must be approved by both Houses by a special majority within one month."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Conversion","q":"In Rev. Stanislaus v. State of Madhya Pradesh (1977), the Supreme Court held that the right to 'propagate' religion under Article 25(1):","o":["includes the right to convert others by any lawful means","is unavailable to religious minorities in any circumstance","does not include a right to convert another person","is not protected by the Constitution at all in any form"],"a":2,"e":"The Court upheld State anti-conversion laws against challenge, holding that 'propagation' means transmission or spreading of one's religion, and not conversion, and that forcible conversion would impinge on the freedom of conscience of others."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Reservation Ceiling","q":"In Indra Sawhney v. Union of India (1992), the nine-judge bench held that reservations under Article 16(4) should ordinarily not exceed:","o":["60 per cent of the posts","75 per cent of the posts","40 per cent of the posts","50 per cent of the posts"],"a":3,"e":"The ceiling may be exceeded only in extraordinary situations, and the Court also upheld the exclusion of the creamy layer among OBCs and held that reservation does not extend to promotions (later modified by the 77th Amendment)."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Maratha Reservation","q":"In Jaishri Laxmanrao Patil v. Chief Minister, Maharashtra (2021), the Supreme Court struck down the Maratha reservation and interpreted which Constitutional Amendment as vesting the power to identify SEBCs for Union purposes?","o":["the 103rd Amendment, which inserted Article 15(6)","the 102nd Amendment, which inserted Article 342A","the 101st Amendment, which inserted Article 279A","the 104th Amendment, which extended Article 334"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that, after the 102nd Amendment, only the President (with Parliament) may identify SEBCs for purposes of Article 342A. The 105th Amendment (2021) later restored the States' power to identify their own lists."},{"s":"Constitutional Law","t":"Right to Know","q":"In Union of India v. Association for Democratic Reforms (2002), the Supreme Court held that voters have a right to know:","o":["the criminal antecedents, assets and education of candidates","the internal party deliberations on selecting candidates","the identity of each and every donor to political parties","the income tax returns of every relative of each candidate"],"a":0,"e":"The right derives from Article 19(1)(a), and was followed by the Representation of the People (Amendment) Act, 2002 and the affidavit requirement for candidates."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Bail","q":"In Satender Kumar Antil v. Central Bureau of Investigation (2022), the Supreme Court classified offences into categories for bail and recommended:","o":["a separate Bail Act, on the lines of the law in the United Kingdom","abolition of anticipatory bail in all non-bailable offences","a ban on bail in all offences punishable with life imprisonment","automatic bail for every accused after six months in custody"],"a":0,"e":"The Court issued guidelines on arrest and bail, stressing that bail is the rule and jail the exception, and cautioned against routine arrests in cases where the offence is punishable up to seven years."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"FIR","q":"Lalita Kumari v. Government of U.P. (2013) held that registration of an FIR is mandatory where the information discloses:","o":["a cognizable offence, but only after prior approval of the Magistrate","any offence at all, whether cognizable or non-cognizable in nature","a cognizable offence, with limited preliminary inquiry in some cases","a serious offence, but only after a full preliminary investigation"],"a":2,"e":"The categories where preliminary inquiry is permissible include matrimonial or family disputes, commercial offences, medical negligence and corruption cases, and delay in reporting."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Stalking, including monitoring a woman's use of the internet, is defined and punished under which section of the BNS?","o":["Section 77","Section 78","Section 74","Section 75"],"a":1,"e":"Section 74 deals with assault or criminal force to outrage modesty, 75 with sexual harassment and 77 with voyeurism."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"The BNS offence corresponding to Section 295A of the IPC (deliberate and malicious acts intended to outrage religious feelings) is Section:","o":["298","196","197","299"],"a":3,"e":"Section 196 deals with promoting enmity between groups, 197 with imputations prejudicial to national integration and 298 with injuring or defiling a place of worship."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Under Section 223 of the BNSS, before taking cognizance of an offence on a complaint, the Magistrate must:","o":["give the accused an opportunity of being heard","issue a public notice in a newspaper to all concerned persons","refer the matter to the police for a final investigation report","obtain the prior sanction of the High Court in every case"],"a":0,"e":"This is a new safeguard not present in the CrPC. The Magistrate must examine the complainant and witnesses on oath and hear the accused before cognizance."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNSS","q":"Section 107 of the BNSS empowers a court, on an application by a police officer, to:","o":["order the preventive detention of a person for a year","suspend the sentence of a convict pending his appeal","attach and forfeit the proceeds of crime","direct the transfer of a criminal case to another State"],"a":2,"e":"The provision strengthens recovery of proceeds of crime in the ordinary criminal process, on the lines of the PMLA, with notice and a hearing to persons affected."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BSA","q":"Under the Bharatiya Sakshya Adhiniyam, 2023, the definition of 'evidence' includes:","o":["only the opinions of experts as recorded by the Magistrate","only oral statements made in open court by the witnesses","statements given electronically, including by witnesses and accused","only documents that are produced in the court in paper form"],"a":2,"e":"This allows examination of witnesses through video-conferencing and treats electronic records as documents, in line with technological developments."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Corruption Law","q":"Section 17A of the Prevention of Corruption Act, 1988 requires prior approval of the competent authority before:","o":["any enquiry into the disproportionate assets of a retired employee","any complaint being made against a public servant to the Lokpal","any investigation into a public servant caught red-handed taking a bribe","an enquiry into a public servant's official recommendation or decision"],"a":3,"e":"Inserted by the 2018 Amendment, the section has been criticised as a hurdle to investigation, and its validity has been challenged in the Supreme Court."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"NDPS","q":"Under Section 37 of the NDPS Act, 1985, bail for offences involving commercial quantity requires the court to be satisfied that:","o":["the accused has deposited an amount that is fixed by the Special Court","there are reasonable grounds to believe he is not guilty and will not reoffend","the accused has been in custody for more than ninety days in aggregate","the prosecution has not yet filed any charge-sheet before the competent court"],"a":1,"e":"These 'twin conditions' also appear in Section 45 of the PMLA and in other special statutes. Courts apply them with an eye to the constitutional guarantee of liberty under Article 21."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"PMLA","q":"In Vijay Madanlal Choudhary v. Union of India (2022), the Supreme Court:","o":["held that the ECIR must be treated as an FIR in all circumstances","upheld the ED's powers of arrest, attachment and twin bail conditions","struck down the twin conditions for bail in Section 45 of the PMLA","held that the Act applies only to scheduled offences before 2002"],"a":1,"e":"The Court held that the Enforcement Case Information Report (ECIR) is an internal document, not an FIR, and that the PMLA stands on a different footing from ordinary criminal procedure; a review is pending."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Criminal conspiracy (earlier Section 120B of the IPC) is punished under which section of the BNS?","o":["Section 49","Section 62","Section 61","Section 45"],"a":2,"e":"Section 45 defines abetment and Section 49 punishes abetment, while Section 62 deals with attempts to commit offences punishable with imprisonment for life or other imprisonment."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Attempt to murder is punishable under which section of the BNS?","o":["Section 103","Section 110","Section 105","Section 109"],"a":3,"e":"Section 110 is attempt to commit culpable homicide, and Section 105 is punishment for culpable homicide not amounting to murder."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"BNS","q":"Under the BNS, an act done by a child under what age is not an offence?","o":["seven years","five years","ten years","twelve years"],"a":0,"e":"Section 20 grants complete immunity to a child below seven, and Section 21 extends a conditional protection to a child between seven and twelve who has not attained sufficient maturity to understand the nature and consequence of the act."},{"s":"Criminal Law (BNS / BNSS / BSA)","t":"Arrest","q":"In Joginder Kumar v. State of U.P. (1994), the Supreme Court held that:","o":["the police may arrest any person on a mere complaint of an offence","the right to inform a relative is available only to accused persons","arrest should not be routine; the arrestee may inform a relative","arrest is mandatory for every offence that is cognizable in nature"],"a":2,"e":"The Court stressed that the power of arrest is different from the justification for its exercise, and that arrest must be reasonable and based on a satisfaction of necessity; the rights have been incorporated into the BNSS."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Frustration","q":"In Satyabrata Ghose v. Mugneeram Bangur & Co. (1954), the Supreme Court held that Section 56 of the Indian Contract Act:","o":["covers events that defeat the contract's object, not only impossibility","applies only where the parties have inserted a force majeure clause","applies only where performance is literally and physically impossible","applies only to contracts for the sale of specific immovable property"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that the doctrine of frustration is part of Section 56 and applies where an unforeseen event renders performance radically different from that undertaken. It is a rule of positive law, not an implied term."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Consideration","q":"Under Section 25 of the Indian Contract Act, an agreement made without consideration is valid if it is:","o":["registered, and made as a gift to any charitable trust or society","oral, and made out of friendship between two persons who are unrelated","in writing, registered, and made out of natural love between near relatives","in writing, but made between two strangers who have agreed to it"],"a":2,"e":"Other exceptions are compensation for past voluntary services and a written, signed promise to pay a time-barred debt."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Agency","q":"A 'del credere' agent is one who:","o":["has been appointed by a court to act as agent for a minor's property","for extra commission, guarantees due performance by the third party","acts without any commission, and bears no liability to the principal","is appointed to act only in a single transaction by the principal"],"a":1,"e":"The agent undertakes to indemnify the principal if the buyer fails to pay, and thereby becomes a surety to that extent."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Medical Negligence","q":"The Bolam test, as laid down in Bolam v. Friern Hospital Management Committee (1957), provides that a professional is not negligent if he acted:","o":["in accordance with the instructions given by the patient to the doctor","in accordance with the latest research published in medical journals","in accordance with the highest standards followed in any part of the world","in accordance with a practice accepted by responsible professional opinion"],"a":3,"e":"The Indian Supreme Court has applied the test with modifications. In Jacob Mathew (2005), it held that a professional is liable for negligence only if he failed to act as a reasonably competent practitioner would."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"State Liability","q":"The distinction between sovereign and non-sovereign functions of the State in tort liability was drawn in India in:","o":["State of Rajasthan v. Vidhyawati (1962)","Rudul Sah v. State of Bihar (1983)","N. Nagendra Rao v. State of Andhra Pradesh (1994)","Kasturilal Ralia Ram Jain v. State of U.P. (1965)"],"a":3,"e":"Kasturilal held that the State was immune for tortious acts committed in exercise of sovereign powers. The scope of the immunity has been narrowed by later cases such as Nagendra Rao, and compensation is available for violations of fundamental rights (Rudul Sah)."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Consumer Law","q":"In Indian Medical Association v. V.P. Shantha (1995), the Supreme Court held that:","o":["paid medical services fall within the Consumer Protection Act","services rendered free of charge are covered by the Act in every case","doctors are excluded from the Consumer Protection Act in every case","only government hospital services are covered by the Act"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that services provided free of charge by government hospitals to all patients are outside the Act, but a service under a contract of personal service is excluded."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Adverse Possession","q":"Under the Limitation Act, 1963, the period for a suit for possession of immovable property based on title against a private person, after dispossession, is:","o":["3 years","30 years","12 years","20 years"],"a":2,"e":"The period of 30 years applies to the Government. Ravinder Kaur Grewal v. Manjit Kaur (2019) held that adverse possession can be asserted as a ground of title and not merely in defence."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Property","q":"The doctrine of part performance under Section 53A of the Transfer of Property Act protects a transferee who:","o":["has obtained a decree of specific performance against the transferor","has taken possession under a written contract and has performed his part","has acquired the property by adverse possession against the true owner","has paid the entire price, but has not taken possession of the property"],"a":1,"e":"The transferee can resist the transferor's attempt to dispossess him, even if the contract was not registered as required, though the 2001 amendment requires registration for such contracts."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Hindu Marriage","q":"Under Section 13(1)(ib) of the Hindu Marriage Act, 1955, desertion is a ground for divorce if it is for a continuous period of not less than:","o":["one year immediately preceding the petition","five years immediately preceding the petition","two years immediately preceding the petition","three years immediately preceding the petition"],"a":2,"e":"Desertion requires the factum of separation and the animus deserendi. Unlike cruelty, it is a continuing offence that must last for the statutory period."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Muslim Law","q":"Mehr (dower) under Muslim law is:","o":["an amount payable as maintenance after the divorce is pronounced","a price paid for the wife's services by the husband to her guardian","a gift given by the wife's father to the husband on the marriage","a sum payable by the husband to the wife as a mark of respect"],"a":3,"e":"Mehr arises from the marriage contract and may be prompt or deferred. If it is not specified, a proper dower is payable."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Succession","q":"Under Section 63 of the Indian Succession Act, 1925, a will must be:","o":["registered with the Sub-Registrar in every case before death","signed by the testator and attested by at least two witnesses","approved by a court before the testator's death takes place","written entirely in the handwriting of the testator himself"],"a":1,"e":"Registration of a will is optional. Each witness must have seen the testator sign or have received his acknowledgment of the signature, and must sign in the testator's presence."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Guardianship","q":"In Githa Hariharan v. Reserve Bank of India (1999), the Supreme Court read the word 'after' in Section 6(a) of the Hindu Minority and Guardianship Act as:","o":["'in the presence of', so both parents must act together as guardians","'with the permission of', so the father's consent is always required","'in the absence of', so the mother may be guardian though father lives","'on the death of', so the mother can act only after the father dies"],"a":2,"e":"The Court avoided striking down the provision as discriminatory by interpreting 'absence' to mean absence of care or interest, and affirmed that the welfare of the minor is paramount."},{"s":"Contract, Torts, Property and Family Law","t":"Maintenance","q":"In Rajnesh v. Neha (2020), the Supreme Court issued guidelines requiring parties in maintenance proceedings to file:","o":["an affidavit of disclosure of assets and liabilities","a bond with sureties, guaranteeing payment of maintenance","a certificate of income issued by the Income Tax Department","a medical certificate on the health of the dependent spouse"],"a":0,"e":"The Court also dealt with interim maintenance, the date from which maintenance is payable (the date of the application) and enforcement of orders."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Inner Morality of Law","q":"Which of the following is NOT one of the eight principles of the 'inner morality of law' identified by Lon Fuller?","o":["Divine origin of the rules","Promulgation of the rules to those affected by them","Non-retroactivity of rules in their application","Congruence between the declared rules and official action"],"a":0,"e":"Fuller's principles are generality, promulgation, non-retroactivity, clarity, consistency, possibility of compliance, constancy and congruence. Their breach produces a failure to make law."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Sociological School","q":"The theory that law is based on 'social solidarity' and the concept of 'public service' is associated with:","o":["Rudolf von Jhering","Hugo Grotius","Max Weber","Leon Duguit"],"a":3,"e":"Duguit rejected the idea of sovereignty as a command, holding that the State's duty is to serve public needs and that law is the product of social interdependence."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Legal Realism","q":"Among the American Legal Realists, the 'fact sceptic' who doubted the reliability of fact-finding in trial courts was:","o":["Hans Kelsen","Karl Llewellyn","Jerome Frank","Roscoe Pound"],"a":2,"e":"Llewellyn is known as a 'rule sceptic', while Frank's Law and the Modern Mind argued that uncertainty comes mostly from the facts of cases."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Public Interest Litigation","q":"The liberalisation of the rule of locus standi, allowing any public-spirited person to seek relief on behalf of those unable to approach the court, was strongly articulated in:","o":["S.P. Gupta v. Union of India (1981)","Golak Nath v. State of Punjab (1967)","A.K. Gopalan v. State of Madras (1950)","Kesavananda Bharati v. State of Kerala (1973)"],"a":0,"e":"In the First Judges Case, the Court held that a person acting bona fide and with sufficient interest can move the court for redress of a legal wrong to those who are poor or disabled."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Natural Law","q":"The modern natural law theory based on 'basic goods' of human flourishing is associated with:","o":["Joseph Raz","Herbert Hart","John Finnis","Hans Kelsen"],"a":2,"e":"In Natural Law and Natural Rights, Finnis argued that practical reasonableness identifies basic goods such as life, knowledge and friendship, which ground a theory of law and rights."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'actio personalis moritur cum persona' means:","o":["a personal action can be transferred by one person to another","a personal action dies with the person","a person's action is always liable to the court's judgment","a personal action may be brought only by the person"],"a":1,"e":"At common law, a cause of action in tort died with the plaintiff or defendant, though the rule has been modified by statute, for example the Indian Succession Act and the Fatal Accidents Act."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Torts","q":"Which case illustrates 'injuria sine damno', that is, a violation of a legal right without actual loss?","o":["Bradford Corporation v. Pickles (1895)","Chasemore v. Richards (1859)","Mogul Steamship Co. v. McGregor Gow & Co. (1892)","Ashby v. White (1703)"],"a":3,"e":"In Ashby v. White, the plaintiff was wrongfully refused the right to vote and succeeded despite his candidate winning. The other cases are examples of damage without a legal injury (damnum sine injuria)."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'sic utere tuo ut alienum non laedas' means:","o":["use your own judgment, as no one can injure you","use your own property so as not to injure another's","let each person use another's property as he chooses","let a person own only what he is able to use himself"],"a":1,"e":"The maxim underlies the law of nuisance and the rule in Rylands v. Fletcher."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'lex non cogit ad impossibilia' means:","o":["the law cannot be changed once it has been laid down","the law applies only to acts that are physically possible","the law compels performance, even if it has become impossible","the law does not compel the performance of an impossible act"],"a":3,"e":"The maxim is reflected in Section 56 of the Contract Act on impossibility and in provisions on the impossibility of compliance as a defence."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Maxims","q":"The maxim 'salus populi suprema lex' means:","o":["the will of the strongest group is the supreme law","the welfare of the people is the supreme law","the safety of the ruler is the supreme law","the custom of the people is above all written law"],"a":1,"e":"The maxim is invoked to justify limits on individual rights for public welfare, as for example in public health and emergency measures."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Res Judicata","q":"The doctrine of res judicata under Section 11 of the Code of Civil Procedure, 1908 bars:","o":["a suit between parties who are strangers to the earlier proceedings","any appeal against a decree passed by a court of competent jurisdiction","the execution of a decree passed in favour of the decree-holder","a fresh trial of a matter directly decided between the same parties"],"a":3,"e":"The doctrine rests on the maxims 'nemo debet bis vexari' (no one should be vexed twice) and 'interest reipublicae ut sit finis litium'. Constructive res judicata extends it to matters that could have been raised."},{"s":"Jurisprudence and Legal Maxims","t":"Separation of Powers","q":"The doctrine of separation of powers into legislative, executive and judicial branches was systematically propounded in The Spirit of the Laws by:","o":["Thomas Hobbes","John Locke","Montesquieu","Jean-Jacques Rousseau"],"a":2,"e":"Montesquieu drew on the English constitution. India follows a flexible separation, which the Supreme Court has treated as part of the basic structure (Kesavananda; Indira Gandhi v. Raj Narain)."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Use of Force","q":"In Nicaragua v. United States (1986), the ICJ held that the United States had:","o":["violated customary law on the use of force and non-intervention","been immune from jurisdiction under its optional clause declaration","acted lawfully in collective self-defence of its ally El Salvador","lacked any obligation under customary international law at that time"],"a":0,"e":"The Court rejected the US plea of collective self-defence and held it responsible for supporting the Contras, with the US later withdrawing its optional clause declaration."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Treaty Law","q":"Under Article 19 of the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties, 1969, a State may NOT formulate a reservation to a treaty if it is:","o":["made after the treaty has been signed but before ratification","made by a State that did not take part in the negotiations","made in a language other than the working language of the treaty","incompatible with the object and purpose of the treaty"],"a":3,"e":"The Court's advisory opinion on Reservations to the Genocide Convention (1951) supplied the compatibility test, which the Vienna Convention codified."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Statehood","q":"Which of the following is NOT a criterion for statehood under Article 1 of the Montevideo Convention on the Rights and Duties of States, 1933?","o":["Recognition by the UN Security Council","A permanent population","Capacity to enter into relations with other States","A defined territory"],"a":0,"e":"The fourth criterion is a government. Under the declaratory theory, statehood does not depend on recognition by other States, which is mainly political."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Nationality","q":"The 'genuine link' requirement for the international opposability of a State's grant of nationality was laid down in:","o":["the Nottebohm case (Liechtenstein v. Guatemala, 1955)","the Lotus case (France v. Turkey, 1927)","the Barcelona Traction case (Belgium v. Spain, 1970)","the Corfu Channel case (UK v. Albania, 1949)"],"a":0,"e":"The ICJ held that Guatemala was not bound to recognise Liechtenstein's diplomatic protection of Nottebohm, as there was no real connection between him and Liechtenstein."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"State Immunity","q":"Under the restrictive theory of State immunity, a foreign State is NOT immune in respect of:","o":["its armed forces' actions during a lawful war","its commercial acts (acta jure gestionis)","its diplomatic functions at a foreign mission","its sovereign acts (acta jure imperii)"],"a":1,"e":"The restrictive theory is reflected in the UN Convention on Jurisdictional Immunities of States and their Property (2004) and in Indian law, in Section 86 of the Code of Civil Procedure."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Law of the Sea","q":"Under Article 17 of UNCLOS, ships of all States enjoy in the territorial sea of a coastal State the right of:","o":["free overflight by aircraft","innocent passage","unrestricted fishing","exclusive resource exploitation"],"a":1,"e":"Passage is innocent so long as it is not prejudicial to the peace, good order or security of the coastal State. Submarines must navigate on the surface and show their flag."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Humanitarian Law","q":"Which principle of international humanitarian law requires parties to a conflict to distinguish between civilians and combatants, and between civilian objects and military objectives?","o":["The principle of distinction","The principle of proportionality","The principle of military necessity","The principle of humanity"],"a":0,"e":"Distinction is a cardinal principle, codified in Additional Protocol I (Article 48) and recognised as customary law by the ICJ in the Nuclear Weapons Advisory Opinion (1996)."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Economic Rights","q":"Article 2(1) of the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights requires each State party to take steps to realise the rights:","o":["immediately, and with full effect in every single case","only in respect of citizens, and not for foreigners","only after developed countries have first realised them","progressively, to the maximum of its available resources"],"a":3,"e":"Some obligations, such as non-discrimination and minimum core obligations, are of immediate effect. India ratified the Covenant in 1979."},{"s":"International Law and Human Rights","t":"Self-Determination","q":"In its 2019 advisory opinion on the Legal Consequences of the Separation of the Chagos Archipelago from Mauritius, the ICJ held that the UK's continued administration of the archipelago is:","o":["lawful, as the Chagossians have consented to the arrangement","lawful, until Mauritius pays compensation for the military base","beyond the Court's jurisdiction as a bilateral matter","an unlawful act that must end as rapidly as possible"],"a":3,"e":"The Court found that the decolonisation of Mauritius was not lawfully completed. In 2025 the UK and Mauritius signed a treaty on transfer of sovereignty, with a lease for Diego Garcia. (Verify its status.)"},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Trade Marks","q":"Under the Trade Marks Act, 1999, a registered trade mark is valid for an initial period of:","o":["5 years, renewable for further periods of 5 years","20 years, renewable for further periods of 20 years","10 years, renewable for further periods of 10 years","7 years, renewable for further periods of 7 years"],"a":2,"e":"Renewal is made on application and payment of the prescribed fee, within six months before the expiry, and a grace period for restoration is available."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Copyright","q":"In the Delhi University photocopying case (Chancellor, Masters & Scholars of the University of Oxford v. Rameshwari Photocopy Services, 2016), the Delhi High Court held that reproduction of course material by a university was:","o":["outside the Copyright Act altogether, as universities are public bodies","covered by the exception for educational purposes in Section 52(1)(i)","permissible, but only if a licence had been obtained from each publisher","infringement, as publishers hold exclusive rights in all forms of use"],"a":1,"e":"The Division Bench held that the expression 'in the course of instruction' covers the reproduction of material for use by students, subject to the extent justified by the purpose."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Patents","q":"The 'Bolar provision' in Section 107A of the Patents Act, 1970 permits:","o":["use of a patented invention to obtain regulatory approval for generics","renewal of a patent beyond the twenty-year term in public interest","sale of any patented product to any buyer without the owner's consent","working of a patent by the Government without paying any royalty"],"a":0,"e":"It allows generic makers to conduct tests and prepare for marketing before patent expiry, and so promotes timely entry of generics."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Aadhaar","q":"In K.S. Puttaswamy (Aadhaar) v. Union of India (2018), the Supreme Court:","o":["struck down Section 57 letting private entities demand Aadhaar","upheld Aadhaar as mandatory for opening every bank account","struck down the entire Aadhaar Act as violative of privacy","held that Aadhaar is mandatory for school admissions and mobile numbers"],"a":0,"e":"The 4:1 majority upheld the Act as a Money Bill and the use of Aadhaar for welfare benefits and for PAN and income tax returns, but read down its use for bank accounts, mobile connections and schools."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Cyber Terrorism","q":"Cyber terrorism under Section 66F of the Information Technology Act, 2000 is punishable with:","o":["imprisonment for a term which may extend to ten years","imprisonment for a term which may extend to seven years","death or imprisonment for life, at the option of the court","imprisonment which may extend to imprisonment for life"],"a":3,"e":"Section 66F covers acts of denying access to a computer resource, unauthorised access or introducing a computer contaminant with intent to threaten the unity, integrity, security or sovereignty of India."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"FEMA","q":"The Foreign Exchange Management Act, 1999, which replaced FERA 1973, treats contraventions as:","o":["criminal offences, which attract imprisonment only","civil wrongs, which attract monetary penalties","offences punishable with death in all serious cases","violations triable only by a Special Court of Sessions"],"a":1,"e":"The shift from FERA's criminal approach to a civil regime followed liberalisation. Adjudication is by officers of the Enforcement Directorate, with appeals to the Appellate Tribunal."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Securities Law","q":"An appeal against an order of the Securities and Exchange Board of India lies to:","o":["the National Company Law Appellate Tribunal","the Securities Appellate Tribunal","the High Court of the State concerned","the Debts Recovery Appellate Tribunal"],"a":1,"e":"Section 15T of the SEBI Act provides for an appeal to the SAT, with a further appeal on a question of law to the Supreme Court under Section 15Z."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Economic Offences","q":"Under the Fugitive Economic Offenders Act, 2018, a person can be declared a fugitive economic offender if he has absconded after committing a scheduled offence involving an amount of at least:","o":["₹200 crore","₹50 crore","₹100 crore","₹500 crore"],"a":2,"e":"The Special Court under the PMLA can declare a person a fugitive economic offender and order confiscation of property, and the person is barred from filing civil claims in India."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Corporate Governance","q":"In Tata Sons Ltd. v. Cyrus Investments Ltd. (2021), the Supreme Court:","o":["set aside the NCLAT order reinstating Cyrus Mistry as Executive Chairman","held that Tata Sons cannot remove a director without the court's leave","directed Tata Sons to be converted into a private limited company","upheld the NCLAT order reinstating Cyrus Mistry as Executive Chairman"],"a":0,"e":"The Court held that the removal did not amount to oppression or mismanagement under Sections 241 and 242 of the Companies Act, 2013 and that the Board's decision was within its powers."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Insolvency","q":"In Pioneer Urban Land and Infrastructure v. Union of India (2019), the Supreme Court upheld the amendment treating homebuyers as:","o":["unsecured creditors, who have no right to initiate insolvency","secured creditors, who may enforce their rights outside the Code","operational creditors, with no say in the Committee of Creditors","financial creditors under the Insolvency and Bankruptcy Code"],"a":3,"e":"Allottees of real estate projects were held to be financial creditors since amounts raised from them have the commercial effect of a borrowing. The 2018 Amendment required at least 100 allottees or 10 per cent to jointly file an application."},{"s":"Commercial, IP, Technology and Corporate Law","t":"Arbitration","q":"In In re: Interplay between Arbitration Agreements under the Arbitration Act and the Stamp Act (2023), a seven-judge bench held that an unstamped arbitration agreement is:","o":["void and unenforceable, so no arbitration may be started","not void, but inadmissible in evidence until duly stamped","valid, if stamped within one year from the date of the dispute","valid and enforceable, with no stamp duty payable at all"],"a":1,"e":"The defect is curable, and the court at the stage of appointment of an arbitrator under Section 11 should only examine the existence of an arbitration agreement. The tribunal deals with stamp issues."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Delegated Legislation","q":"In Hamdard Dawakhana v. Union of India (1960), the Supreme Court held that the legislature cannot delegate:","o":["its essential legislative function, such as laying down policy","its power to make any rules or regulations of any kind","its power to commence an Act on a date fixed by the executive","its power to fix details of its application to the executive"],"a":0,"e":"Excessive delegation, where the legislature abdicates by leaving policy to the executive, is unconstitutional, though conditional legislation and delegation of details are permissible."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Reasoned Decisions","q":"In S.N. Mukherjee v. Union of India (1990), the Supreme Court held that administrative authorities:","o":["must record reasons for decisions, subject to statutory exceptions","must record reasons only in decisions imposing a financial penalty","need not record reasons, even for decisions that are quasi-judicial","must record reasons only when the court directs them to do so"],"a":0,"e":"The requirement ensures fairness, helps the courts exercise judicial review and reduces arbitrariness, and is now regarded as an aspect of natural justice."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Tribunals","q":"Which Article of the Constitution empowers Parliament to provide for Administrative Tribunals for adjudication of disputes relating to recruitment and conditions of service of public servants?","o":["Article 323B","Article 262","Article 323A","Article 227"],"a":2,"e":"Article 323A (inserted by the 42nd Amendment) led to the Administrative Tribunals Act, 1985, while Article 323B deals with tribunals on other subjects such as taxation and industrial disputes."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Wildlife","q":"Animals listed in Schedule I of the Wild Life (Protection) Act, 1972 receive:","o":["regulated hunting, with a licence from the Collector","protection only if they are found in a national park","classification as vermin, which may be killed freely","the highest protection, with the severest penalties"],"a":3,"e":"Schedule II also gets high protection, while Schedule V lists vermin. The 2022 Amendment rationalised the schedules and added provisions for CITES compliance."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Hazardous Substances","q":"The Public Liability Insurance Act, 1991 provides for:","o":["no-fault liability and immediate relief for victims of hazardous accidents","liability only for accidents occurring in State-owned industrial units","exemption from liability when the accident is caused by a stranger's act","fault-based liability, needing proof of negligence by the owner concerned"],"a":0,"e":"Owners handling hazardous substances must take out insurance and contribute to an Environmental Relief Fund. The Act followed the Bhopal tragedy and the M.C. Mehta decisions."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Environmental Law","q":"The principle of 'polluter pays' and absolute liability for harm caused by hazardous industries was applied to chemical industries in Bichhri village in:","o":["M.C. Mehta v. Kamal Nath (1997)","Subhash Kumar v. State of Bihar (1991)","Indian Council for Enviro-Legal Action v. Union of India (1996)","Rural Litigation and Entitlement Kendra v. State of U.P. (1985)"],"a":2,"e":"The Court directed the polluting industries to bear the cost of remedial measures to restore the damaged ecology, and ordered recovery of costs from them."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Atrocities Act","q":"Section 18A of the SC/ST (Prevention of Atrocities) Act, 1989, inserted in 2018 and upheld in Prathvi Raj Chauhan v. Union of India (2020), provides that:","o":["a preliminary inquiry is necessary before every FIR is registered","anticipatory bail is available in all cases, subject to leave of court","a public servant may be arrested only after his employer approves","no preliminary inquiry before an FIR, and no anticipatory bail"],"a":3,"e":"Parliament overrode the 2018 Dr. Subhash Kashinath Mahajan decision, which had required a preliminary inquiry and approval before arrest. The Court noted that anticipatory bail may be granted if no prima facie case is made out."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Forced Labour","q":"In People's Union for Democratic Rights v. Union of India (Asiad Workers' case, 1982), the Supreme Court held that payment of wages below the minimum wage amounts to:","o":["a breach of contract punishable under the Indian Penal Code","a lawful form of labour, if the worker has agreed to it","a matter solely for the State Governments to regulate","forced labour prohibited by Article 23"],"a":3,"e":"The Court held that 'forced labour' under Article 23 includes labour extracted for less than minimum wages, since the economic compulsion leaves the worker no real choice."},{"s":"Administrative, Environmental and Social Legislation","t":"Child Marriage","q":"Under the Prohibition of Child Marriage Act, 2006, the minimum age of marriage for a woman and a man, respectively, is:","o":["18 years and 18 years","16 years and 18 years","18 years and 21 years","21 years and 21 years"],"a":2,"e":"The Prohibition of Child Marriage (Amendment) Bill, 2021, which seeks to raise the age for women to 21, is pending. In Society for Enlightenment and Voluntary Action v. Union of India (2024), the Court issued guidelines stressing prevention and protection over mere punishment."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Census","q":"The reference date for the population enumeration of the upcoming Census, which will for the first time include caste enumeration, is:","o":["1 March 2026","1 March 2027","1 January 2026","1 October 2027"],"a":1,"e":"The Census is to be conducted in two phases, houselisting and population enumeration, and is relevant to women's reservation and delimitation under the 106th Amendment. (Verify the dates in the Census notification.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Tax Law","q":"The Income-tax Act, 2025, which replaces the Income-tax Act, 1961, comes into effect from:","o":["1 January 2026","1 April 2026","1 April 2027","1 April 2025"],"a":1,"e":"The new Act, passed in August 2025, simplifies language and restructures provisions and replaces the concepts of 'previous year' and 'assessment year' with a single 'tax year'. (Verify before the exam.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Immigration Law","q":"The Immigration and Foreigners Act, 2025 replaces how many earlier laws, including the Foreigners Act, 1946 and the Passport (Entry into India) Act, 1920?","o":["four","three","five","two"],"a":0,"e":"The four laws replaced are the Passport (Entry into India) Act, 1920, the Registration of Foreigners Act, 1939, the Foreigners Act, 1946 and the Immigration (Carriers' Liability) Act, 2000. (Verify before the exam.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Sports Law","q":"The National Sports Governance Act, 2025 provides for the establishment of:","o":["a National Sports University and a National Sports Fund","a National Olympic Authority and a National Sports Ombudsman","a National Sports Board and a National Sports Tribunal","a National Sports Police and a National Sports Court"],"a":2,"e":"The Act aims to improve governance and transparency of national sports federations and to provide a tribunal for sports disputes. (Verify its provisions before the exam.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Aviation Law","q":"The Bharatiya Vayuyan Adhiniyam, 2024 replaced which Act?","o":["The Indian Aircraft Rules, 1937","The Aircraft Act, 1934","The Carriage by Air Act, 1972","The Airports Authority of India Act, 1994"],"a":1,"e":"The Adhiniyam is a part of the Government's effort to replace colonial laws and modernise aviation regulation."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Anti-Corruption","q":"The first Chairperson of the Lokpal, appointed in 2019, was Justice Pinaki Chandra Ghose. Who was appointed Chairperson of the Lokpal in 2024?","o":["Justice Sanjay Kishan Kaul","Justice D.Y. Chandrachud","Justice Hemant Gupta","Justice A.M. Khanwilkar"],"a":3,"e":"Justice Ajay Manikrao Khanwilkar, a former Supreme Court judge, was appointed in February 2024. (Verify the current Chairperson before the exam.)"},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Judiciary","q":"The sanctioned strength of the Supreme Court of India, including the Chief Justice, is:","o":["33 judges","37 judges","34 judges","31 judges"],"a":2,"e":"The Supreme Court (Number of Judges) Amendment Act, 2019 raised the strength from 31 to 34. Article 124(1) leaves the number to be fixed by Parliament."},{"s":"Contemporary Legal Developments and Legal GK","t":"Transparency","q":"The Right to Information (Amendment) Act, 2019 was criticised because it:","o":["extends the time limit for supplying information from 30 to 60 days","lets the Centre fix the tenure and salaries of Information Commissioners","exempts the Prime Minister's Office from the Act in every respect","removes the Central Information Commission from the Act in its entirety"],"a":1,"e":"Earlier, the Act fixed the tenure at five years and the salary equal to that of the Chief Election Commissioner. The amendment is seen as weakening the independence of the Commissions."}],"b":[{"id":"S1","m":25,"w":450,"q":"Preventive and procedural safeguards on arrest and bail, from Joginder Kumar and D.K. Basu to Pankaj Bansal and Satender Kumar Antil, have been incorporated in the BNSS. Critically evaluate whether the BNSS adequately protects personal liberty under Articles 21 and 22.","o":["Constitutional baseline: Articles 21 and 22(1)-(2), procedure established by law after Maneka Gandhi.","Judicial safeguards: Joginder Kumar, D.K. Basu, Arnesh Kumar, Pankaj Bansal (written grounds), Antil (bail guidelines).","BNSS features: notice of appearance (Section 35), e-FIR and Zero FIR, police custody over 40/60 days, Section 479 undertrial release, Section 223 hearing before cognizance.","Concerns: extended police custody, handcuffing, attachment of proceeds of crime, uneven implementation.","Conclusion: provisions are broadly aligned with constitutional safeguards, but outcomes depend on judicial oversight and training of police."]},{"id":"S2","m":25,"w":450,"q":"The Supreme Court has upheld the ED's powers under the PMLA (Vijay Madanlal Choudhary) while also stressing that bail is the rule even under stringent statutes. Discuss the tension between effective enforcement against economic offences and the constitutional guarantee of liberty.","o":["PMLA framework: scheduled offences, proceeds of crime, Sections 3, 5, 19, 45 and 50.","Vijay Madanlal Choudhary (2022): ECIR is not an FIR, twin conditions upheld, arrest and attachment powers valid.","Liberty jurisprudence: Pankaj Bansal on written grounds, Manish Sisodia and Senthil Balaji on delay and bail, Article 21 and speedy trial.","Counter-arguments: gravity of money laundering, international obligations (FATF), difficulty of proving laundering.","Conclusion: reconcile through proportionality, timely trials, and reasoned arrests subject to judicial review."]}]};
